      "narration": "Everything in Part Six was about the model itself. This part is about the gap between what the model knows and what your application needs, and that gap is where most real systems live. Start with the shape of the problem. A model knows what was in its training data, up to a cutoff date, and that is the entirety of it. It cannot tell you what your company's refund policy says. It cannot tell you what happened last week. It cannot tell you anything about a private document, a customer record, an internal wiki, or a codebase it never saw. And this is not a limitation that goes away with a bigger model — a frontier model has exactly the same blind spot about your documents as a small one, because the issue is not capability, it is that the information was never present. Now the dangerous part, and it is why this matters more than it sounds. If you ask anyway, you do not get an error. You get a fluent, well-structured, confident answer that is invented. The model has no internal signal separating I have read this from this is the kind of thing that would be true, because both produce the same kind of high-probability text. Confabulation and recall are the same operation from the inside. So the failure is silent and it looks exactly like success, which is the worst property a failure can have. We already ruled out one fix. In the LoRA lesson I said: fine-tune behaviour, retrieve knowledge, and here is the reasoning in full. Facts baked into weights are undated — there is no way to know when they were true. They are unattributable — you cannot show a user the source, which for anything regulated or consequential is disqualifying on its own. They are unchangeable without retraining, so correcting a single sentence of a policy document is a training run. And they are unverifiable, because you cannot inspect what the model absorbed. Every one of those is fatal for a system that has to be trusted. The right answer is retrieval-augmented generation, and I want to state it plainly because people expect something more elaborate. Find the documents relevant to the question. Paste them into the prompt. Ask the question about them. That is RAG. The division of labour is clean and it is the whole insight: the model provides language understanding and reasoning, which is what it is extraordinarily good at, and you provide the facts, which is what you actually control. Nothing is baked in, everything is current, and every answer can point at the passage it came from.",
