      "heading": "Search by meaning, not by keyword",
      "narration": "Finding the relevant documents is the hard half, and keyword search is not good enough for a reason you can see immediately. A user types how do I get my money back. Your policy document says refund eligibility criteria. Those two strings share no words at all, and they are about exactly the same thing. Keyword search returns nothing, or worse, returns something irrelevant that happened to share a common word. This is not an edge case; it is the ordinary case, because people ask questions in their own words and documents are written in the organization's words. Which is where this course pays off, because you already have the tool. In Part One you learned that direction in a vector space can carry meaning and that cosine similarity measures it. In Part Five you learned that a BERT-family encoder produces a contextual representation of a whole passage. Put those together: run an embedding model over every chunk of your documents, store the resulting vectors, then embed the user's question the same way and find the stored vectors closest to it by cosine similarity. Get my money back and refund eligibility land near each other in that space, because they appear in similar contexts, which is the distributional hypothesis doing exactly what it promised. Nothing new is required — this is the machinery from Parts One and Five, applied. A vector database is the infrastructure around that. FAISS if you want a library, Chroma for something simple, pgvector if you already run Postgres and would rather not add a system, Pinecone or similar if you want it managed. What they all do is store millions of vectors and answer nearest-neighbour queries in milliseconds. And they do it approximately, by design — exact nearest-neighbour search over ten million vectors is slow, so these systems use index structures like HNSW that find almost-certainly-the-nearest results a hundred times faster. That is a deliberate accuracy-for-speed trade and it is essentially always correct here, because you are going to retrieve five candidates and let the model sort them out. Now the decision that determines your system's quality more than the database choice does, and that people spend the least time on: chunking. You cannot embed a fifty-page document as one vector — a single vector cannot represent fifty pages, and what you get is a blurred average of everything in it that matches nothing specifically. So you split. Chunk too large and the embedding mixes several topics and retrieval becomes vague. Chunk too small and you get a sentence with no context, and the model cannot use it. The practical advice: split on structural boundaries first — sections, headings, paragraphs — rather than on a fixed character count, because a chunk that ends mid-argument is a chunk that cannot answer anything. Aim for a few hundred tokens, overlap neighbouring chunks by ten or twenty percent so a fact spanning a boundary is not lost, and keep the source and section with every chunk so you can cite it later.",
