      "heading": "Why the first version disappoints, and what fixes it",
      "narration": "Naive RAG is a weekend project, and the first version usually underwhelms. The good news is that the ways it fails are well understood and mostly have specific fixes. The most important diagnostic first, and it will save you weeks. When answers are bad, almost everyone starts editing the prompt. Do not. Print the chunks that were retrieved and read them. The overwhelming majority of the time you will find that the passage containing the answer was never fetched at all, and in that case there is no prompt in the world that helps — the model was asked to answer from material that did not contain the answer, and it did the best it could, which is either to say it does not know or to invent something. Retrieval is the bottleneck, not the model, and the whole debugging discipline follows from taking that seriously. Now the specific failures. Pure vector search has a real blind spot: it is bad at exact matches. Product codes, error numbers, function names, unusual proper nouns, version strings. Embeddings capture meaning, and an error code has no meaning to capture — it is a token that either appears or does not. So run keyword search, BM25, alongside the vector search, and fuse the two ranked lists. That is hybrid search, it takes an afternoon, and on most corpora it is the single largest quality improvement available. Second, reranking, which is the highest-value thing most people have not done. Embedding similarity is a fast approximation: the question and the chunk were embedded separately, so nothing ever compared them directly. A cross-encoder does compare them directly — it reads the query and the chunk together and scores the pair — and it is far more accurate and far too slow to run over a million documents. So do both: retrieve fifty candidates cheaply with vector search, then rerank those fifty with the cross-encoder and keep the best five. You pay for fifty comparisons instead of a million, and the five chunks that reach the prompt are much better chosen. Third, the queries themselves. Users write short, vague, context-dependent questions, and a follow-up like what about for annual plans is meaningless as a standalone search. So rewrite the query before searching: have a small model expand it using the conversation history, or generate two or three phrasings and retrieve for all of them. Cheap, and it fixes the multi-turn case that otherwise falls apart. And finally, evaluate the two stages separately, because a single end-to-end score tells you nothing actionable. Measure retrieval by whether the correct chunk appears in the top-k, on a set of questions where you know the answer's location. Measure generation by faithfulness: given the chunks that were retrieved, is the answer actually supported by them, or did the model add something. Those are different failures with different fixes, and a team that reports one blended quality number will spend its time guessing. Build the evaluation set first — fifty questions with known source passages — and the rest of the work becomes measurable instead of anecdotal.",
